        ],
        notes="Source: original course 2.1 slides 7–9 and https://hadoop.apache.org/docs/r3.2.2/hadoop-mapreduce-client/hadoop-mapreduce-client-core/MapReduceTutorial.html . Start by asking attendees to predict where the two books records must meet. Advance one step at a time. Input split A/B are teaching examples; an HDFS block is not automatically one input split. Map tasks process independently; shuffle transfers and sorts intermediate pairs so equal keys reach the same reducer. Groups shown do not imply one reducer per key. Omit combiners for clarity. Map output is buffered/spilled locally, not necessarily written to HDFS. The HDFS write shown is job output between jobs. MapReduce also provides task retries and fault tolerance; Spark did not invent those. End with why this is useful AND costly for iterative or interactive reuse. No claim that all disk use disappears in Spark.",
    )

pub def slide_spark_performance() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="spark-performance",
        title="Hadoop worked, but it was slow",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Hadoop worked, but it was slow", level=1),
            prose("Run another calculation on the same data.", style="subtitle"),
            group([
            node(NodeKind.OrderedList, children=[
                node(NodeKind.ListItem, content="Read the data"),
                node(NodeKind.ListItem, content="Use it again"),
                node(NodeKind.ListItem, content="Keep it in memory"),
            ], style="lesson-rail"),
            group([
                group([
                    span(""),
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="FIRST CALCULATION"),
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="SECOND CALCULATION"),
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="THIRD CALCULATION"),
                ], style="reuse-head"),
                group([
                    rich_heading([
                        text("Hadoop"),
                        line_break(),
                        text("MapReduce"),
                    ], level=2),
                    group([
                        span("Read from disk", style="disk-read"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                    ]),
                    group([
                        span("Read again", style="disk-read"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                    ], style="repeat-step"),
                    group([
                        span("Read again", style="disk-read"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                    ], style="repeat-step"),
                ], style="reuse-row"),
                group([
                    heading("Spark", level=2),
                    group([
                        span("Read from disk", style="disk-read"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                        span("Persist in memory", style="memory-store"),
                    ]),
                    group([
                        span("Reuse from memory", style="memory-reuse"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                    ], style="warm-step"),
                    group([
                        span("Reuse from memory", style="memory-reuse"),
                        span("Compute", style="work-step"),
                    ], style="warm-step"),
                ], style="reuse-row spark-reuse"),
                prose("Same reusable working set · persisted partitions fit and remain in memory · schematic, not a timing chart", style="reuse-assumption"),
            ], style="reuse-board"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Paragraph, children=[
                    strong("Both start by reading the data"),
                    text("The first load still has a cost. Spark does not make the underlying disk faster."),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-cue", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Paragraph, children=[
                    strong("What if we need that same data again?"),
                    text("Separate MapReduce jobs read their input again. What could Spark avoid?"),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-cue", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Paragraph, children=[
                    strong("Read once. Reuse in memory."),
                    text("With persistence, later calculations can skip rereading and rebuilding the same working set."),
                ], attributes=[attr("data-cue", flag=true)]),
            ], style="lesson-cues"),
            group([
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Previous point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-prev", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Next point", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-next", flag=true)]),
                node(NodeKind.Button, content="Replay", attributes=[attr("data-lesson-reset", flag=true)]),
                span("", style="lesson-position", attributes=[attr("aria-live", "polite")]),
            ], style="lesson-controls"),
        ], style="lesson reuse-comparison", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "2")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("28"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="The comparison is repeated calculations over a reusable working set, not every Hadoop or Spark workload. First show both reading from disk: we are not claiming Spark disk reads are inherently faster, nor asserting identical measured I/O speeds. Advance to Hadoop reading again, then pause: What could Spark do differently? Reveal persistence and the two memory reuse steps. Once computed and retained, Spark can avoid rereading and recomputing these partitions. This is the in-memory story behind the historical performance gains; no numeric speedup is asserted. Hadoop/OS caching can also affect physical disk I/O. Spark still uses disk for initial input and may use it for shuffle, spill, persistence or recovery. Reuse here assumes explicitly persisted partitions fit and remain in memory. Transition: how can distributed data be retained and recovered safely? That leads to RDDs. Source: https://spark.apache.org/docs/0.7.0/scala-programming-guide.html#rdd-persistence",
    )

pub def slide_spark_speedup() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="spark-speedup",
        title="How much faster?",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide speed-evidence",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("How much faster?", level=1),
            prose("A historical example: logistic regression in Hadoop and Spark.", style="subtitle"),
            group([
            node(NodeKind.Figure, children=[
                group([
                    heading("Hadoop", level=2),
                    group([
                        span("", attributes=[attr("style", "width:100%")]),
                    ], style="speed-track"),
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="110 s"),
                ], style="speed-row"),
                group([
                    heading("Spark", level=2),
                    group([
                        span("", attributes=[attr("style", "width:0.8181818%")]),
                    ], style="speed-track"),
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="0.9 s"),
                ], style="speed-row spark-speed"),
                node(NodeKind.FigureCaption, content="Running time · seconds · lower is better"),
            ], style="speed-bars", attributes=[attr("aria-label", "Reported running time: Hadoop 110 seconds, Spark 0.9 seconds. Bars use the same linear scale.")]),
            group([
                strong("≈122×"),
                prose("faster in this example"),
                span("110 ÷ 0.9"),
            ], style="speed-ratio"),
        ], style="speed-layout"),
            prose("Repeated work is where reusing data can make a dramatic difference.", style="speed-bridge"),
            paragraph([
            text("Source: "),
            node(NodeKind.Link, content="Apache Spark · MLlib performance example", attributes=[attr("href", "https://spark.apache.org/mllib/"), attr("target", "_blank"), attr("rel", "noopener")]),
            line_break(),
            text("Historical result, not a general speed guarantee. Loading costs and test setup are not specified on the source page."),
        ], style="speed-source"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("29"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Use after the in-memory explanation. Ask: How big a difference can this make? Apache Spark’s own MLlib page shows Hadoop 110 seconds and Spark 0.9 seconds for logistic regression. The ratio calculated from those labels is approximately 122.2. This recovers the figures from the original course, using a primary source instead of the rejected July 2013 PySpark talk. Explain that iterative algorithms reuse a working set, so the previous slide explains an important reason such gains are possible. Do not claim this graphic isolates persistence, measures only warm iterations, includes or excludes initial loading, or benchmarks the Python API specifically: the page and image do not specify those details, versions, hardware or dataset size. No benchmark date is asserted. This is a historical illustration, not a current benchmark or a promise for every workload. If asked for a production estimate, explain that reuse, memory capacity, I/O, shuffle and workload all matter. End with: But, how does Spark do this? Pause, then advance to the RDD-only slide. Do not expand the acronym yet. Sources: https://spark.apache.org/mllib/ and https://spark.apache.org/images/logistic-regression.png . Source image retained in assets/history/spark-logistic-regression-source.png.",
    )

pub def slide_rdd_prompt() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="rdd-prompt",
        title="RDD",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide rdd-prompt",
        content=[
            heading("RDD", level=1),
        ],
        notes="Let the acronym sit on screen. Ask: Who has come across RDDs? Can anyone tell me what RDD stands for? How might it relate to the performance difference we just saw? Pause and invite answers before advancing. The next slide expands the acronym and explains partitioned data, persistence and recovery. Keep the distinction clear: RDDs support this model, but reuse across calculations requires persistence; merely creating an RDD does not cache it.",
    )

pub def slide_rdd_foundation() -> Slide:
    steps = [
        TeachingStep(label="Source",
            heading="A different way to reuse the same sales",
            explanation="The source stays available. Spark describes partitioned collections and their transformations.",
            cue_key="0", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Partitions",
            heading="Distributed · one collection, multiple partitions",
            explanation="Parse the records into RDD partitions. This is a plan until an action requests computation.",
            cue_key="1", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Persist",
            heading="Reuse · keep a useful intermediate RDD",
            explanation="After it is computed, persist these partitions for later calculations. Here they fit in memory.",
            cue_key="2", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Reuse",
            heading="Another calculation, the same persisted data",
            explanation="Sum and count can reuse the parsed records rather than read and parse the source again.",
            cue_key="3", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Loss",
            heading="Resilient · a partition becomes unavailable",
            explanation="The stored copy of P0 is lost. P1 remains available; the source and lineage remain.",
            cue_key="4", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Recover",
            heading="Lineage · rebuild the missing partition",
            explanation="Read source P0 and replay its parse. This narrow dependency lets us rebuild only P0.",
            cue_key="5", numbered=true),
        TeachingStep(label="Why?",
            heading="Pause · why does this matter?",
            explanation="Partitions explain parallel work. Dependencies explain required work. Recovery can explain repeated work.",
            cue_key="6", numbered=true),
    ]
    return Slide(
        slug="rdd-foundation",
        title="Resilient Distributed Dataset",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide ",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
