        ], style="lesson rb-lesson", attributes=[attr("data-lesson", flag=true), attr("data-point", "2")]),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("35"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Original corpus: Section 2, 2.4 Spark Dataframes, slide 10. Rebuilt as a three-step assignment walkthrough. First establish that df refers to A. Then highlight and evaluate the right-hand expression: select returns B, while df still refers to A. Finally highlight the left-hand name and move the single df marker to B. Only one current binding is ever shown. A and B stay fixed so the audience can see that A's columns never change. The boxes depict DataFrame descriptions and schemas, not materialised rows or a complete physical execution plan. A and B are teaching labels, not Spark IDs. Do not use df.rdd.id() to establish DataFrame identity; it identifies an RDD and is unavailable in Spark Connect. The snippet keeps no separate Python name for A after assignment; B retains a plan derived from its input. Keeping A in this illustration explains the derivation, not a guarantee about object lifetime. Ask: have we actually processed the rows yet? Carry that question through the API overview and recap into Lazy Pandas. Manual Next/Previous/Replay controls let the instructor pause between evaluation and assignment. Static, print and reduced-motion views show the final binding and the complete explanation. Reference: https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/api/python/reference/pyspark.sql/api/pyspark.sql.DataFrame.select.html",
    )

pub def slide_api_overview() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="api-overview",
        title="How the APIs fit together",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide lesson-slide",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("How the APIs fit together", level=1),
            prose("Different ways to describe work. The same distributed foundations.", style="subtitle"),
            node(NodeKind.Figure, children=[
            group([
                heading("RDD API", level=2),
                prose("Elements + your functions"),
                group([
                    text("↓"),
                ], style="api-down"),
                prose("You specify the transformations", style="api-direct-caption"),
            ], style="api-direct"),
            group([
                group([
                    group([
                        heading("DataFrame", level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("Named columns + expressions"),
                            line_break(),
                            node(NodeKind.Bold, content="Our PySpark interface"),
                        ]),
                    ]),
                    node(NodeKind.Small, children=[
                        node(NodeKind.Link, content="Reference: typed Datasets in Scala / Java ↗", attributes=[attr("href", "core-abstractions-reference.html#dataset-context")]),
                    ]),
                ], style="api-family"),
                group([
                    text("↓"),
                ], style="api-down"),
                group([
                    node(NodeKind.Bold, content="Spark SQL / Catalyst"),
                    span("Logical plan → optimised physical plan"),
                ], style="api-planner"),
                group([
                    text("↓"),
                ], style="api-down"),
            ], style="api-structured"),
            group([
                strong("Distributed execution · RDDs underneath"),
                span("Partitions   →   tasks on executors   ·   dependencies   ·   recovery"),
            ], style="api-runtime"),
        ], style="api-map", attributes=[attr("aria-label", "RDD operations go to distributed execution. DataFrame operations go through Spark SQL planning and optimisation before distributed execution.")]),
            group([
            group([
                strong("groupBy / join"),
                span("May move data between partitions: a shuffle."),
            ]),
            group([
                strong("One slow partition"),
                span("Uneven work can hold up the whole stage."),
            ]),
            group([
                strong("Use the result again"),
                span("Persistence can avoid repeating earlier work."),
            ]),
        ], style="api-why"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("36"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="Recap the two interfaces in this course: RDDs and DataFrames. Start at DataFrame: we describe what we want using schema-aware expressions. Catalyst analyses and optimises that query and produces a physical plan. Spark SQL uses internal row/columnar representations and RDD-based execution beneath those operators. Compare the left RDD API path: users supply transformations directly; arbitrary functions do not give Catalyst equivalent relational information. Do not call a DataFrame merely an RDD with column names. Both paths rely on partitions and dependencies and run tasks on executors. That is why learning the lower layer helps diagnose a shuffle, skew, repeated computation and recovery. A DataFrame can be created from an RDD plus a schema, but need not originate from a Python RDD. No manual conversion to RDD is needed. In classic PySpark, df.rdd exposes Python Rows; moving to arbitrary Python RDD transformations leaves Spark SQL relational optimisation for those transformations. Spark Connect does not support the RDD API. If asked about the reference link: Spark 2.0 unified DataFrame/Dataset in Scala/Java; Scala DataFrame is Dataset[Row], Java uses Dataset<Row>. Python has no typed Dataset[T] API. Source: https://spark.apache.org/releases/spark-release-2-0-0.html ; https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/sql-programming-guide.html ; https://github.com/apache/spark/blob/v4.0.0/sql/core/src/main/scala/org/apache/spark/sql/execution/SparkPlan.scala . End by pointing to Catalyst: “This is Catalyst, Spark’s query optimiser. I’ll come back to that.” Then say “But first…” and advance to the Lazy Pandas cover. Leave the execution question open for the audience.",
    )

pub def slide_abstractions_recap() -> Slide:
    return Slide(
        slug="abstractions-recap",
        title="Core abstractions: recap",
        frame=Frame.Composed,
        styles="slide chapter-recap",
        content=[
            group([
            text("03 / SPARK’S CORE ABSTRACTIONS"),
        ], style="eyebrow"),
            heading("Core abstractions: recap", level=1),
            node(NodeKind.Table, children=[
            node(NodeKind.Caption, content="Core abstractions: recap: key takeaways", style="sr-only"),
            node(NodeKind.Columns, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Column),
                node(NodeKind.Column),
            ]),
            node(NodeKind.TableBody, children=[
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="RDDs", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Immutable, partitioned collections. Lineage records how their partitions were derived."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="DataFrames", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Transformations describe new DataFrames. Named columns and expressions help Spark SQL optimise the work."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Persistence", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Computed partitions can be kept for reuse. Defining an RDD or DataFrame does not cache it."),
                ]),
                node(NodeKind.Row, children=[
                    node(NodeKind.HeaderCell, content="Recovery", attributes=[attr("scope", "row")]),
                    node(NodeKind.Cell, content="Lost RDD partitions can be recomputed from their dependencies, while the required input remains available."),
                ]),
            ]),
        ], style="chapter-recap-table"),
            prose("A DataFrame is still distributed data: its rows are processed in partitions.", style="chapter-recap-takeaway"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("37"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Return to the same sales and the recovered P0. Immutability applies to transformations: select creates a new DataFrame description, and reassignment changes a Python binding. It does not eagerly duplicate rows. Reuse avoids work only while the persisted data is available. Recovery depends on available input and recomputable lineage; it is not a backup of arbitrary external changes. Typed Datasets remain a Scala/Java reference topic.

https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/rdd-programming-guide.html
https://spark.apache.org/docs/4.2.0/sql-programming-guide.html""",
    )

